  "prompt": "Your friend is trying to understand the strategy used to prove the approximation theorem. They summarized the procedure as follows, but they don't understand the missing steps or why they are necessary. Fill in the blanks (___) and explain the purpose of each step:\n\n1. First, treat the case of one-dimensional inputs and approximate the function using rectangles.\n2. Next, generalize this rectangle construction to multidimensional inputs, resulting in hyperrectangles.\n3. Then, show that a two-layer ReLU network can approximate a hyperrectangle (___).\n4. Finally, use the third layer of the network to linearly combine all the rectangles (___).",
  "reference_answer": "The first step allows the function to be approximated by rectangles, which can be quantified easily. The second step generalizes this construction to multidimensional inputs, creating hyperrectangles. The third step is necessary to establish the fundamental link between the geometric shape (the hyperrectangle) and the network architecture, proving that a two-layer ReLU network can approximate it. The final step uses the third layer to linearly combine all these individual rectangles, which allows the network to approximate the original function.",
