{
  "prompt": "Explain, in your own words, the relationship between network depth, width, and the number of kinks in a deep ReLU network, and what this implies for function approximation.",
  "reference_answer": "The core mechanism is that applying ReLU can at most double the number of kinks present in the input function. Formally, the maximum number of kinks ($KINK_L$) grows at most polynomially in width ($n$), but exponentially in depth ($L$). This exponential growth in depth suggests a significant benefit to making the network deeper. Therefore, if the goal is to approximate functions that contain many kinks, it is better to design a network that is deeper rather than one that is wider.",
  "key_points": [
    {
      "text": "ReLU can at most double the number of kinks.",
      "keywords": ["doubling effect of the ReLU", "at most double"]
    },
    {
      "text": "The upper bound grows polynomially in width.",
      "keywords": ["polynomially in width", "upper bound grows"]
    },
    {
      "text": "The upper bound grows exponentially in depth.",
