  "prompt": "Explain the relationship between finding the optimal set of parameters ($\\theta^*$) and encountering a local minimum during gradient descent. How do they differ, and when might a local minimum prevent finding the true optimum?",
  "reference_answer": "The ultimate goal of gradient descent is to find the optimal value, $\\theta^*$, which represents the parameters that minimize the cost function. However, when traversing the loss landscape, if this landscape is non-convex and complex, the algorithm may not be guaranteed to find the global minimum. Instead, the process might settle into a local minimum. While the optimal set of theta is the ideal lowest point, a local minimum is simply a point where the gradient is zero, but it may not be the best possible solution.",
  "key_points": [
